data.content:
## What Breaks in .NET 11 (EF Core 11)

The **Cosmos DB provider removes all synchronous LINQ/SaveChanges APIs entirely**. In EF Core 10 you could opt in to sync-over-async with:

```csharp
options.UseCosmos(..., o => o.AllowSynchronousIO());
```

In EF Core 11, that opt-in is **gone**. Calling `ToList()`, `First()`, `Count()`, or `SaveChanges()` on the Cosmos provider now throws an **`InvalidOperationException`** at runtime — there is no configuration to re-enable it.

## Fix: Convert to Async

```csharp
public class ProductRepository
{
    private readonly CosmosDbContext _context;

    public async Task<List<Product>> GetAllAsync()
    {
        return await _context.Products.ToListAsync();
    }

    public async Task<Product> GetByIdAsync(int id)
    {
        return await _context.Products.FirstAsync(p => p.Id == id);
    }

    public async Task<int> GetCountAsync()
    {
        return await _context.Products.CountAsync();
    }

    public async Task SaveAsync(Product product)
    {
        _context.Products.Add(product);
        await _context.SaveChangesAsync();
    }
}
```

**Key points:**
- `Add()` stays synchronous (it's an in-memory tracking call, not a DB call).
- All **query execution** (`ToList`, `First`, `Count`, etc.) and **`SaveChanges`** must become async.
- Callers must propagate `async/await` up the call chain (controllers, services, etc.).
